WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:41.200
I don't have anything close to a daily&nbsp;
routine, per say. Every morning is different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.880
and I'm completely unable to follow&nbsp;
through with most repetitive practices.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:54.080
Most of my days are work days where very little&nbsp;
interesting happens. However, now and then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:00.160
when I'm all caught up, a day off is in&nbsp;
order. A day reserved to rise with the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:07.920
to simply be, to do exactly what I love to do&nbsp;
for every minute of it. These days are holy ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:14.320
in which time loses its meaning. If you have&nbsp;
ever experienced a day where you forget about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:25.840
the passing hours due to pure enjoyment of what&nbsp;
you're doing, you know how special it can be.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:46.080
When I spend enough time wandering the cascades,&nbsp;
I lose myself. Maybe I simply stepped into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:52.640
fairy circle, but suddenly everything happens&nbsp;
at a gentle pace. There's no ticking clock to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:58.400
remind me of the hours that rule our lives. I'm&nbsp;
completely connected to a world of my own design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:05.360
relishing the beauty of the alpine flowers. I&nbsp;
wander and wander, and mutter to myself as random&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:11.840
thoughts go through my head. For example, wouldn't&nbsp;
the yellow bells make fantastic hats for fairies?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:16.880
And with that, suddenly it's the&nbsp;
evening and the day has passed me

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.840
by.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:53.360
I once read a book about a little boy.&nbsp;
He lived very long ago when there were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.040
few people on this earth. He was part of a&nbsp;
tribe who lived by the sun and the seasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.360
These people didn't have minutes or seconds.&nbsp;
The child saw this and thought that he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:10.800
help his people if he measured the days to better&nbsp;
predict the future: the best days for hunting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:17.600
the best days for berry picking. He invented&nbsp;
a crude version of a sundial, and he marked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:24.000
the hours. This new knowledge excited him, and&nbsp;
soon he began to mark shorter periods of time...

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.240
As a grown man he marked the minutes, as a&nbsp;
white bearded elder he marked the seconds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:38.080
And suddenly his final day on earth presented&nbsp;
itself, and he wept that he had never realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:43.600
that despite how he counted his days he had&nbsp;
not stopped to think that his days were already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:50.480
counted. He had let them slip through his fingers.&nbsp;
And with his final breath he warned his people to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:59.840
be careful of how they use the knowledge&nbsp;
of time, lest it rule over their lives.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:45.360
I read the story, initially, when I was a child&nbsp;
and unfortunately I can't remember the book I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:51.360
read it in. But that little boy stayed with me,&nbsp;
there's so many sayings in my culture that pertain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:58.240
to time. Words like “be efficient, don't waste&nbsp;
your time” “time is money” “you're losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:02.880
time” or, perhaps, the most terrifying:&nbsp;
“don't waste the best time of your life”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.240
It gives time an enormous power over&nbsp;
us, as if we need to fear misusing it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.360
I don't think this is necessarily&nbsp;
wrong, as our lives are very precious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.480
but I do think that sometimes we put an&nbsp;
overly high value on being productive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.840
and using our time in a way that achieves a goal&nbsp;
such as having a highly productive morning routine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:31.440
that I absolutely cannot follow through with, no&nbsp;
matter how hard I try. In fact, maybe just sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:37.840
or thinking or not doing anything particular at&nbsp;
all can be a very productive use of our time.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:54.800
Advertisements often suggest ways to&nbsp;
save time and live more efficiently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:59.520
as if that should be our ultimate goal,&nbsp;
after spending a day wandering the mountains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:02.240
I think I've had one of the&nbsp;
most productive days of my life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.600
and I have nothing to show for it except a&nbsp;
pinecone and the speckled shell of a quail's egg.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:14.800
But I felt truly free, and know that I didn't&nbsp;
waste one second simply enjoying the day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:22.160
I hope you take some time to simply forget what&nbsp;
you're doing tomorrow, or the day after, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:44.080
disconnect from the clock. You may be surprised&nbsp;
by what you find. Have a lovely day or night 🤍

